#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# range

#% text
# `range(n)` gives the list of the integers 1, 2, ..., `n`.

#% text
# `range(a, b)` gives `a`, `a` + 1, ... up to `b`.

#% text
# `range(a, b, step)` gives `a`, `a` + `step`, `a` + 2 `step`, ... as long as the values do not pass `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The start and the step default to 1. The end is included when the steps land on it exactly.

#% item
# The bounds and the step may be integers, fractions or machine reals; with a machine-real step the entries are computed afresh for each position, so long ranges do not accumulate rounding errors.

#% item
# A negative step counts down. An empty range (an end before the start for the direction of the step) gives `[]`.

#% item
# A step of 0, or bounds that are not real numbers, give a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
range(5)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

#% cell
range(3, 8)
#% output
#= [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

#% cell
range(0, 1, 1/4)
#% output
#= [0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1]

#% cell
range(10, 1, -3)
#% output
#= [10, 7, 4, 1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Machine-real steps:

#% cell
range(0, 1, 0.25)
#% output
#= [0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0]

#% text
# The last value stays at or before the end:

#% cell
range(1, 10, 4)
#% output
#= [1, 5, 9]

#% text
# A range that is empty for the direction of its step:

#% cell
range(5, 1)
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Sampling points for a table of values:

#% cell
table([x, x^2], x, range(0, 1, 1/2))
#% output
#= [[0, 0], [1/2, 1/4], [1, 1]]

#% text
# The sum of the odd numbers below 20:

#% cell
sum(range(1, 19, 2))
#% output
#= 100

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A range of `n` integers has `n` elements:

#% cell
length(range(1000))
#% output
#= 1000

#% text
# Reversing a range gives the range with the opposite step:

#% cell
reverse(range(1, 9, 2)) === range(9, 1, -2)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A step of zero cannot reach the end:

#% cell
range(1, 5, 0)
#% output
#! failure(argument, range, 3)

#% text
# Bounds that are exact numeric expressions rather than numbers leave the call as it is; use a range of integers and scale it:

#% cell
range(0, pi, pi/4)
#% output
#= [0, (1/4)*pi, (1/2)*pi, (3/4)*pi, pi]

#% cell
range(0, 4)*pi/4
#% output
#= [0, (1/4)*pi, (1/2)*pi, (3/4)*pi, pi]

#% text
# A symbolic bound cannot be expanded:

#% cell
range(n)
#% output
#= range(n)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `table`, `array`, `take`, `reverse`
